NAME
    dma - inspect DMA/HDMA channel state and transfer logs

SYNOPSIS
    dma
    dma log on [<n>]
    dma log off
    dma log [<n>]
    dma log clear
    dma stats

DESCRIPTION
    Inspects the block-transfer (DMA and HDMA) channels of the attached
    core and optionally records transfer history and bandwidth statistics.

    Bare 'dma' prints every channel's current or most recent configuration:
    direction, B-bus write pattern, named target register, source address,
    transfer length, and for HDMA the table pointer and line counter. On
    SNES cores using the stable ABI, the channel table is decoded from the
    engine's IO space ($4300-$437F plus MDMAEN and HDMAEN); enabled HDMA
    channels show 'on' while the line counter is nonzero and 'arm' once it
    reaches zero.

    Transfer logging ('dma log') requires transfer-event reporting from
    the core; cores that do not emit per-transfer records refuse 'dma log'.
    When supported, 'dma log on' records each transfer (channel, kind,
    destination, source, length, frame, and scanline) into a bounded ring
    buffer while keeping whole-run counters for 'dma stats'.

OPTIONS
    log on [<n>]
        Start recording DMA/HDMA transfers into a ring retaining up to <n>
        entries (default 4096).

    log off
        Stop recording transfers while preserving the ring and totals.

    log [<n>]
        Print the last <n> recorded transfers in chronological order
        (default 20).

    log clear
        Clear the transfer ring and bandwidth totals.

    stats
        Print per-channel (split into general DMA and HDMA) and per-
        destination-register transfer totals, ordered largest first.

EXAMPLES
    dma
    dma log on
    dma log 40
    dma stats

EXIT STATUS
    0   Command completed (or usage/unsupported message printed).
    1   No target attached, unsupported transfer log, invalid argument, or
        session service fault.

SEE ALSO
    bp(1), copflow(1), mem(1), watch(1), tilemap(1)
